Well, I think I found a work-around :-)

I already mentioned I'm not a programmer, and the work-around may seem
like a bit of a bold move, but for now, it seems to work.

So, reading back my 'dmesg' I was intrigued by the line saying:
pci: pyxis 8K boundary dma bug - sg dma disabled
This sort of felt like the worst part of the bad news. I found back this
string in /usr/src/linux/arch/alpha/kernel/sys_miata.c :

static void __init
miata_init_pci(void)
{
        cia_init_pci();
        /* The PYXIS has data corruption problem with scatter/gather
           burst DMA reads crossing 8K boundary. It had been fixed
           with off-chip logic on all PYXIS systems except first
           MIATAs, so disable SG DMA on such machines. */
        if (!SMC669_Init(0)) {  /* MIATA GL has SMC37c669 Super I/O */
                alpha_mv.mv_pci_tbi = NULL; 
                printk(KERN_INFO "pci: pyxis 8K boundary dma bug - "
                                 "sg dma disabled \n");
        }
        es1888_init();
}

And I interpret this as that the alpha_mv.mv_pci_tbi pointer is set to NULL
whenever a SMC37c669 chip cannot be initialized, which means we're not
dealing with a MiataGL, so it must be an old style Miata.

So, my bold move was to just change that one line into
/*              alpha_mv.mv_pci_tbi = NULL; */
which effectively skips setting the pointer to NULL.

I realise this is not smart nor elegant; it may break other stuff or
seriously reduce reliability of what-not...
Still, rebuilding the kernel and booting it went fine, as did modprobing
emu10k1.
Now I finally have my SBlive working with a 2.4 kernel :-)
Last half hour I tried sort of stress-testing it by running a lot of
stuff simultaneously, like Windowmaker, galeon, mozilla-news,
setiathome, gkrellm, XMMS, a 2.4.19-rc2 compilation and an xterm with
top. So the system was fully loaded. No appearant problems yet
discovered....

For completeness sake, I might as well mention what hardware I use in
this system:
PWS433 (Miata)
Compaq scsi-card ripped from an old Proliant, containing an Symbios
Logic 53c875 with 2 Compaq (Seagate) ST34371W 40MB/s harddrives
NEC 466 scsi cdrom
Archive 4326 scsi tape-drive
3Dfx Voodoo3 3000 pci videocard
SoundBlaster Live (emu10k1) card (the cheaper model)
I use the 3 32-bit PCI-slots and none of the 64-bit ones.
I don't use IDE disks or atapi-cdrom, so I leave that out of the
kernel-configuration completely (I don't use the CMD646 at all).

This is all on Debian GNU/Linux - Sid (unstable, that is!).

If anyone could suggest a better, safer or more elegant solution, I
would be happy to hear it. Also, if anyone has any clues to what things
I might have broken with the work-around, please let me know.
I will be more than happy trying/testing stuff that you might suggest.

Thanx!

Dannis.


